  "title": "Kociewie Villages with Embroidery",
  "lead": "Colorful embroidery, wooden churches, and villages where traditional holidays are still celebrated. A region next to Kashubia. Slow travel through Kociewie villages.",
  "body": "<h2>Why Kociewie villages, differently</h2><p>Kociewie is an ethnographic region located south of Kashubia, in Pomerania. Though less known than Kashubia, it has an equally rich folk culture — colorful embroidery, wooden churches, traditional holidays and customs that have survived to this day. Kociewie villages are peaceful, clean, and full of rural charm. This is one of the few places in Poland where folklore is not a tourist attraction but a living part of the daily life of residents.</p><p>Slow travel through Kociewie villages is a journey through culture and tradition. Instead of rushing from village to village, stop in one longer — talk to residents, enter the church, see Kociewie embroidery, listen to how the village lives. Each village has its own history, its own churches, its own inhabitants. This is a journey that rewards those who slow down — the longer you are in one place, the more you can truly understand about Kociewie culture.</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Kociewie villages split into several groups: villages with wooden churches (Pelplin and surroundings), villages with Kociewie embroidery (Tczew, Starogard), villages with traditional farmsteads (Kociewie Ethnographic Park), villages in the Tuchola Forest. Exploring the region takes 1–2 days.</p><h3>Villages with wooden churches</h3><ul><li><strong>Pelplin</strong> — a small town with a Gothic cathedral, one of the most important in Poland. In the surrounding villages, wooden churches have survived, including the church in Wysoka from the 18th century.</li><li><strong>Wysoka village</strong> — a wooden church from the 18th century, one of the most valuable monuments of wooden architecture in the region.</li><li><strong>Pinczyn village</strong> — a wooden church from the 18th century, with interesting interior furnishings.</li><li><strong>Królów Las village</strong> — a wooden church from the 18th century, less known but worth attention.</li></ul><h3>Villages with Kociewie embroidery</h3><ul><li><strong>Kociewie embroidery</strong> — colorful patterns (flowers, birds, geometric motifs) decorate not only folk costumes today but also everyday objects. Workshops in Tczew, Starogard, and other towns.</li><li><strong>Tczew</strong> — a town with a Kociewie embroidery tradition. There are several workshops where you can see the embroidery process.</li><li><strong>Starogard Gdański</strong> — a town with a Kociewie embroidery tradition. There are several workshops and a regional museum.</li><li><strong>Villages with embroidery</strong> — several villages in the region have preserved the Kociewie embroidery tradition. Pay attention to cottages with embroidered elements.</li></ul><h3>Kociewie Ethnographic Park and Tuchola Forest</h3><ul><li><strong>Kociewie Ethnographic Park</strong> — an open-air museum in Wdzydze Kiszewskie (formally Kashubian, but also presenting Kociewie culture). Worth it for context.</li><li><strong>Villages in the Tuchola Forest</strong> — villages scattered in the Tuchola Forest, with wooden architecture and forest tradition.</li><li><strong>Ocypel village</strong> — a village in the Tuchola Forest, with forest tradition and wooden architecture.</li><li><strong>Wda village</strong> — a village on the Wda river, with kayaking tradition and wooden architecture.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Fewer tourists, long days, stable weather. Worth a walk around the villages.</li><li><strong>July–September</strong> — season for craft festivals and fairs. Worth it for cultural events.</li><li><strong>September–October</strong> — second best. Autumn colours, fewer people. September is a good time for mushroom picking in the Tuchola Forest.</li><li><strong>November–March</strong> — villages in winter are less accessible (some workshops closed). Worth it for those who want to see the villages without crowds.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~40 minutes from Gdańsk (A1), ~1.5 hours from Bydgoszcz (DK91), ~3.5 hours from Warsaw (S7 and A1).</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> Tczew, Starogard Gdański, Pelplin have railway stations, with connections from most Polish cities. Then rent a car or bike.</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS and private operators serve most villages.</li></ul><p>Kociewie villages are best explored by car or bike — they are scattered over a large area, and public transport between them can be sparse. Plan the route to connect 3–4 villages in one day.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p>A weekend (1–2 days) is the minimum — Tczew or Starogard Gdański (Kociewie embroidery, wooden churches) and surroundings.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Tczew, Starogard Gdański, Pelplin, villages in the Tuchola Forest.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore all Kociewie villages — from Tczew to the Tuchola Forest, with numerous stops.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Kociewie soup</strong> — a thick soup with vegetables, groats, and meat, available at village inns.</li><li><strong>Baltic herring in oil</strong> — a coastal cuisine classic, available at fish shops.</li><li><strong>Kaszanka with onion</strong> — a simple, filling dish, available at inns and markets.</li><li><strong>Kociewie krupnioki</strong> — groat sausage, available in local shops.</li><li><strong>Kociewie bread</strong> — sourdough bread, available at bakeries in Tczew and Starogard.</li><li><strong>Beer from local breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region.</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Tczew (town, more options), Starogard Gdański (town, more options), Pelplin (small town, smaller choice), agritourism farms in villages (quiet, home-cooked food).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Guided tours</strong> — the history of Kociewie villages is complex, and without context many sites look similar. Take a guide at selected places.</li><li><strong>Craft workshops</strong> — many workshops offer courses (Kociewie embroidery, cutouts, sculpture). Book ahead.</li><li><strong>Shopping</strong> — buy directly from craftsmen. It is not only a souvenir but also support for vanishing trades.</li><li><strong>Opening hours</strong> — many workshops are open only on selected days. Check hours before your trip.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — weather in the region can be changeable. Even in July, bring a rain jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good in towns, weaker in villages. Have an offline map.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Visit Kociewie during Easter or Christmas — traditional holidays are celebrated here in a unique way, with local customs.",
